# Accepted findings, each with the reason it is accepted. Nothing here is
# suppressed because it was noisy; anything not listed still fails the build.
#
# Reviewed 2026-09-23.

# AWS-0104 - unrestricted security group egress.
# Both VMs must reach the internet to install packages and pull container
# images, and the Wazuh VM does so through the app VM's NAT hop. Pinning
# egress would mean enumerating apt, Docker Hub, GitHub and the AWS APIs by
# address, which change without notice. Inbound is where this lab is actually
# restricted: exactly one port, udp/51820, is reachable from the internet.
AWS-0104

# AWS-0132 - S3 bucket not encrypted with a customer-managed key.
# The state and manifest buckets use SSE-S3 (AES256). A customer-managed key
# costs roughly $1/month each and adds key policy management for no meaningful
# gain in a short-lived lab whose budget is trial credit. Both buckets block
# all public access and deny non-TLS requests.
AWS-0132

# KSV-0014 - container root filesystem is writable.
# Juice Shop keeps its SQLite database on local disk and the OWASP CRS nginx
# image writes to its cache and temp directories at runtime. Making these read
# only requires mapping each writable path to an emptyDir, which is worth
# doing but was not attempted late in the build rather than risking an
# unverified change to a working, evidenced stack. Recorded as an incomplete
# item in the README.
KSV-0014

# KSV-0118 - default security context permits root.
# Both containers already set allowPrivilegeEscalation: false, and Juice Shop
# additionally drops all capabilities. Adding runAsNonRoot requires confirming
# the UID each upstream image actually runs as; guessing it produces a pod
# that will not start. Same reasoning as KSV-0014 - recorded rather than
# rushed.
KSV-0118
